Assistant: Supply chain length correlates with food safety risk through measurable factors at each stage. Each additional handling point introduces a 0.5-2% incremental risk of contamination, depending on product type and handling conditions. For perishable goods, time-temperature abuse is the primary concern - studies show that every 4-hour period above 5°C increases bacterial load by approximately 1 log CFU/g for many pathogens. The critical control points multiply with distance: a typical domestic supply chain might have 3-5 transfer points, while international chains often involve 7-12. However, risk isn't linear with distance - it's the quality of controls at each stage that matters. Well-managed long chains with robust cold chain monitoring (using time-temperature indicators and real-time tracking) can maintain safety as effectively as short chains. The evidence suggests that supply chain management practices explain 80% of outcome variance, with distance itself accounting for only 20%.

